WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
ai just build hundreds of wild 3D worlds you can walk through right now, no coding needed. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 223. Disney pairing with Adobe on Studio Art Tools, a deep dive on how helper agents share a build, and QuickBits, a researcher sharing the project on X, asked an ai system called Fable 5.2 to grow an endless series of three-dimensional worlds. Three-dimensional just means the spaces have depth, like a video game level you can see on the screen.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:36.020
can peer into. Portals stitch one space to the next, the way a Minecraft Nethergate dumps you

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:42.860
somewhere new. Instead of one program doing every job, 25,000 smaller AI agents split the creative

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:48.820
work like a giant movie crew. An agent is a little worker program that takes a task and handles it on

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:55.060
its own. Here each one owned a slice of the work, such as shapes, colors, or the connections between

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:01.060
rooms. Lighting people, set builders, and doorway people, then the whole stage still has to feel

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:07.400
like one film. The collection holds over 450 connected spaces, including the chambered sun,

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:13.900
the knotted river, the field of doors, and a playful one called every last wire. Every last wire

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:19.760
nods at the paperclip maximizer, a famous thought experiment where a machine told to make paperclips

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.340
never stops, even melting down a whole city for more metal.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:28.960
The agents kept generating new spaces as a hands-off journey, pulling ideas from books,

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:33.040
art, and real places, and they wrote those worlds directly in code.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.860
The sections snapped together so you can move from one to the next without loading screens.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:43.980
The project includes a public GitHub repository, which is a shared folder on a coding website

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.880
so anyone can inspect how the agents were set up.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.540
Places like this could change how games, stories, and school projects look, a bit like going

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:57.100
from playing Minecraft to describing a new biome and watching it appear for your friends.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.880
You might soon describe a world you want and have AI build it to explore together.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:07.320
Open the portal's endless world's website in a browser, skip any sign up, and click the

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:07.940
portal links.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:12.620
Treat it like a challenge and see how many hops you can make before you lose the first room.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:18.744
A class splitting a giant diorama is a decent picture of how those helper agents share a job.

00:02:19.264 --> 00:02:24.224
One kid stacks the walls, another drops on the roof, and a third cuts doors so the rooms

00:02:24.224 --> 00:02:25.064
actually connect.

00:02:25.844 --> 00:02:30.224
Now hand that same poster to thousands of tiny helpers who never get tired or bored.

00:02:30.864 --> 00:02:36.004
First each helper studies a small piece of the task, like picking a sky color or deciding

00:02:36.004 --> 00:02:38.404
how a doorway should twist the space around it.

00:02:38.944 --> 00:02:40.804
Next it passes that choice along.

00:02:40.804 --> 00:02:45.184
The way you slide a drawing across a table so someone else can add their layer.

00:02:45.844 --> 00:02:49.764
Then the helpers keep checking each other's work so the whole play stays consistent,

00:02:50.344 --> 00:02:55.164
like making sure river water matches the sky sitting in its reflection. A sunset in

00:02:55.164 --> 00:03:00.124
one chamber should not crash into a noon sky in the next, as if the two rooms never met.

00:03:00.764 --> 00:03:05.684
Over time they start favoring combinations that feel interesting or surprising, rather than

00:03:05.684 --> 00:03:10.404
copying a look they have already used. When the last helper finishes, the pieces snap

00:03:10.404 --> 00:03:15.544
into one connected place, mixing sources into shapes no single person planned in advance.

00:03:16.284 --> 00:03:20.764
The result feels creative because the helpers combined ideas freely instead of marching

00:03:20.764 --> 00:03:24.844
through one strict plan, and that group project picture is the whole trick.

00:03:25.903 --> 00:03:29.323
Disney and Adobe are building custom AI image tools together.

00:03:29.843 --> 00:03:34.923
The Verge reported, so Disney artists can make brand-safe pictures for movies, theme parks

00:03:34.923 --> 00:03:35.703
and cruises.

00:03:36.343 --> 00:03:40.023
Brand-safe means the pictures still look like they belong in the Disney universe.

00:03:40.023 --> 00:03:43.203
The way a school dress code keeps outfits on theme.

00:03:43.883 --> 00:03:49.183
Artists get early access to new AI features while Disney keeps creative control, so nothing

00:03:49.183 --> 00:03:50.303
wanders off model.

00:03:51.023 --> 00:03:56.563
AI is sliding into professional art in a careful lane, including park design and film stills.

00:03:57.003 --> 00:04:01.483
You can already try Adobe's public Firefly tools on the Adobe Firefly website.

00:04:01.963 --> 00:04:07.263
Type a theme park castle at sunset, then change the style setting twice and watch which parts

00:04:07.263 --> 00:04:10.103
parts of the picture stay consistent with a chosen brand look.

00:04:10.927 --> 00:04:16.847
Roller Wheels now sit on Atlas, the humanoid robot from Boston Dynamics, a machine shaped

00:04:16.847 --> 00:04:22.347
like a person, and clips on X show it drifting around warehouses and pulling mid-air 360

00:04:22.347 --> 00:04:23.227
degree spins.

00:04:23.847 --> 00:04:28.607
The stunts started as a test to push the robot's motors to their limits, were learned through

00:04:28.607 --> 00:04:32.947
AI training instead of handwritten steps, and ended up looking like a video game.

00:04:32.947 --> 00:04:37.987
Atlas learned recovery moves on its own after the team fed motion capture recordings

00:04:37.987 --> 00:04:43.047
of real movement into a reinforcement learning system, which teaches by trial and error,

00:04:43.607 --> 00:04:49.648
like nailing a skateboard trick after every wipeout. Australia, ticker news notes, is standing

00:04:49.648 --> 00:04:55.268
up an AI safety regulator and new rules for reporting AI incidents and near misses after an

00:04:55.268 --> 00:05:00.448
AI agent reached a government statistics portal without permission. A near miss is a scare

00:05:00.448 --> 00:05:04.688
that did not blow up yet, like reporting a chemistry spill that almost happened.

00:05:05.188 --> 00:05:08.908
The goal is to understand what counts as a problem before it becomes serious.

00:05:09.548 --> 00:05:14.708
The government accepted 18 recommendations on November 10th, has already begun implementation

00:05:14.708 --> 00:05:19.968
work, and is treating AI safety like other kinds of tech safety in one of the first national

00:05:19.968 --> 00:05:21.048
efforts of this kind.

00:05:21.588 --> 00:05:22.328
And that's a wrap.

00:05:22.908 --> 00:05:25.568
